{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PZk94aanTkGra2DmPsyPfx","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t// Copyright (c) 2026 Petar Djukic. All rights reserved.\n2\t// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\n3\t\n4\t// Package testutils provides the differential testing harness for go-unix-utils.\n5\t// It executes a Go binary and a GNU reference binary with identical inputs and\n6\t// compares stdout, stderr, and exit code.\n7\t// Implements srd001-testutils.\n8\tpackage testutils\n9\t\n10\timport (\n11\t\t\"bytes\"\n12\t\t\"context\"\n13\t\t\"errors\"\n14\t\t\"fmt\"\n15\t\t\"os\"\n16\t\t\"os/exec\"\n17\t\t\"path/filepath\"\n18\t\t\"regexp\"\n19\t\t\"strings\"\n20\t\t\"testing\"\n21\t\t\"time\"\n22\t)\n23\t\n24\tconst defaultTimeout = 10 * time.Second\n25\t\n26\t// NormalizeFunc transforms raw output bytes before comparison.\n27\t// R1.4: named type alias for normalization functions.\n28\ttype NormalizeFunc = func([]byte) []byte\n29\t\n30\t// DiffTest defines a single differential test case.\n31\t//\n32\t// R1.1: Name identifies the subtest (used with t.Run). Args holds command-line\n33\t// arguments passed to both binaries.\n34\t//\n35\t// R1.2: Stdin nil means both binaries receive EOF immediately. An empty non-nil\n36\t// slice ([]byte{}) also produces no bytes but represents an open stdin that is\n37\t// immediately closed. ExitCode defaults to 0 when left as the zero value.\n38\t//\n39\t// R1.3: Env nil means both binaries inherit the test process environment with\n40\t// LC_ALL=C applied as a default override. When non-nil, KEY=VALUE pairs are\n41\t// merged into the inherited environment: matching keys are overridden, new keys\n42\t// are added.\n43\t//\n44\t// R1.4: Normalize holds a slice of NormalizeFunc applied in order to stdout and\n45\t// stderr of both binaries before comparison. Nil or empty means no normalization.\n46\ttype DiffTest struct {\n47\t\tName          string\n48\t\tArgs          []string\n49\t\tStdin         []byte\n50\t\tEnv           []string\n51\t\tWorkDir       string\n52\t\tExitCode      int\n53\t\tNormalize     []NormalizeFunc\n54\t\tExpectedFiles map[string][]byte\n55\t}\n56\t\n57\tvar tsPatterns = []*regexp.Regexp{\n58\t\tregexp.MustCompile(`[A-Z][a-z]{2}\\s+\\d{1,2}\\s+\\d{2}:\\d{2}:\\d{2}`),\n59\t\tregexp.MustCompile(`\\d{4}-\\d{2}-\\d{2}\\s+\\d{2}:\\d{2}:\\d{2}`),\n60\t\tregexp.MustCompile(`\\d{2}:\\d{2}:\\d{2}`),\n61\t}\n62\t\n63\t// TimestampNormalizer replaces common strftime timestamp patterns with a fixed\n64\t// placeholder string.\n65\t// R4.2: built-in normalizer for cmd/ts tests.\n66\tvar TimestampNormalizer NormalizeFunc = func(b []byte) []byte {\n67\t\tresult := b\n68\t\tfor _, pat := range tsPatterns {\n69\t\t\tresult = pat.ReplaceAll(result, []byte(\"<TIMESTAMP>\"))\n70\t\t}\n71\t\treturn result\n72\t}\n73\t\n74\ttype binaryResult struct {\n75\t\tstdout   []byte\n76\t\tstderr   []byte\n77\t\texitCode int\n78\t}\n79\t\n80\t// RunDiffTests runs each DiffTest as a named subtest, executing goBinary and\n81\t// refBinary with identical inputs and comparing their outputs.\n82\t// R2.1: accepts paths to Go and reference binaries plus test cases.\n83\tfunc RunDiffTests(t *testing.T, goBinary, refBinary string, tests []DiffTest) {\n84\t\tt.Helper()\n85\t\tfor _, tc := range tests {\n86\t\t\tt.Run(tc.Name, func(t *testing.T) {\n87\t\t\t\trunSubtest(t, goBinary, refBinary, tc)\n88\t\t\t})\n89\t\t}\n90\t}\n91\t\n92\tfunc runSubtest(t *testing.T, goBinary, refBinary string, tc DiffTest) {\n93\t\tt.Helper()\n94\t\tworkDir := tc.WorkDir\n95\t\tif workDir == \"\" {\n96\t\t\tworkDir = t.TempDir()\n97\t\t}\n98\t\tenv := buildEnv(tc.Env)\n99\t\tgoRes := runBinary(t, goBinary, tc.Args, tc.Stdin, env, workDir)\n100\t\trefRes := runBinary(t, refBinary, tc.Args, tc.Stdin, env, workDir)\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_011dQUTosrocWcSdkyzm6ypq","session_id":"134b6010-6885-4f08-8e6f-190349c7e60e","uuid":"b553bacb-fcf0-4233-ae61-47df04af33a5","timestamp":"2026-05-29T15:30:07.910Z"}
