{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017b2hwYf9nxF9PNfzUaKfSJ","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tid: go-unix-utils\n2\ttitle: \"go-unix-utils Architecture\"\n3\t\n4\toverview:\n5\t  summary: |\n6\t    go-unix-utils is a pipeline from C-source-derived specifications to verified Go\n7\t    binaries. Each utility follows the same four-stage flow: extract, specify, synthesize,\n8\t    verify. The synthesis stage is driven by mage-claude-orchestrator, an automated code\n9\t    generation system. Specification development is human-guided. A shared pkg/ library\n10\t    accumulates common logic as utilities are added.\n11\t  lifecycle: |\n12\t    For each utility a human operator clones the C source repository, analyzes flags and\n13\t    edge cases, and uses the make-work and do-work Claude Code skills to produce two\n14\t    specification artifacts: a SRD (numbered requirements and acceptance criteria covering\n15\t    all flags, exit codes, and stream behaviors) and a use case (concrete end-to-end\n16\t    execution path through the system). The SRD is the primary input to code generation.\n17\t    Once the spec artifacts are complete, the human invokes the mage-claude-orchestrator\n18\t    via mage targets; the orchestrator drives a Claude agent to generate Go code in\n19\t    cmd/<utility>/ and runs the differential testing harness. If tests fail, the operator\n20\t    refines the SRD and the orchestrator re-generates.\n21\t\n22\t    Shared library components (pkg/sys, pkg/format, pkg/testutils) may also have SRDs\n23\t    when their requirements are non-trivial and span multiple utilities.\n24\t  coordination_pattern: |\n25\t    The human operator owns the specification phase. The mage-claude-orchestrator owns\n26\t    the synthesis and verification phases. Mage targets (magefiles/orchestrator.go)\n27\t    expose the boundary between them: the operator calls mage targets to hand off to\n28\t    the orchestrator after the spec is ready. The differential testing harness in\n29\t    pkg/testutils/ is the shared acceptance criterion for both phases.\n30\t\n31\tinterfaces:\n32\t  - name: Data Structures\n33\t    spec_file: docs/interfaces/ifc-data-structures.yaml\n34\t  - name: Events\n35\t    spec_file: docs/interfaces/ifc-events.yaml\n36\t\n37\tcomponents:\n38\t  - name: Human Operator\n39\t    responsibility: |\n40\t      Guides specification development for each utility. Clones the C source, analyzes\n41\t      flags and edge cases, and uses the make-work and do-work Claude Code skills to\n42\t      produce a SRD and a use case. Decides when the spec artifacts are complete enough\n43\t      to hand off to the orchestrator. Refines SRDs when orchestrator-generated code\n44\t      fails differential tests. Also authors SRDs for shared pkg/ components when their\n45\t      requirements warrant it.\n46\t    capabilities:\n47\t      - make-work skill breaks a utility into specification issues\n48\t      - do-work skill works through individual specification issues\n49\t      - C source analysis via cloned coreutils/moreutils/grep/findutils repositories\n50\t    references:\n51\t      - .claude/commands/\n52\t\n53\t  - name: Specification Repository\n54\t    responsibility: |\n55\t      Holds the formal per-utility SRDs and use cases alongside this documentation suite.\n56\t      Serves as the source of truth for synthesis and verification. Shared library\n57\t      components (pkg/) may also have SRDs.\n58\t    capabilities:\n59\t      - One SRD per utility under docs/specs/software-requirements/ (plus SRDs for shared pkg/ components as needed)\n60\t      - One use case per utility under docs/specs/use-cases/\n61\t      - VISION.yaml and ARCHITECTURE.yaml project-level docs\n62\t      - Test suites under docs/specs/test-suites/\n63\t    references:\n64\t      - docs/\n65\t\n66\t  - name: cmd/\n67\t    responsibility: |\n68\t      Holds the Go implementation of each utility. Each sub-package compiles to a\n69\t      standalone binary and contains its differential tests alongside the implementation.\n70\t      cmd/ grows as utilities are implemented following road-map.yaml. The full catalog\n71\t      of 123 target utilities (coreutils, moreutils, grep, findutils) is in\n72\t      docs/utilities.yaml; stdbuf is the only utility in those repos that is explicitly\n73\t      excluded (LD_PRELOAD injection not possible in pure Go).\n74\t    capabilities:\n75\t      - One package per utility, named cmd/<utility>/\n76\t      - Each package contains main.go (implementation) and <utility>_test.go (differential tests)\n77\t      - Active roadmap in road-map.yaml for current release and use case status\n78\t      - Full catalog in docs/utilities.yaml for difficulty, memory model, and implementation profile of each utility\n79\t    references:\n80\t      - cmd/\n81\t\n82\t  - name: pkg/sys/\n83\t    responsibility: |\n84\t      Wraps Darwin and Linux syscalls and signal handling. Provides a stable interface\n85\t      that cmd/ packages use to avoid platform-specific code in utility implementations.\n86\t      Only syscalls that have platform divergence between Darwin and Linux, or that Go's\n87\t      standard library does not expose cleanly, belong here.\n88\t    capabilities:\n89\t      - Terminal size queries (TIOCGWINSZ ioctl)\n90\t      - Signal handling (SIGPIPE, SIGHUP, SIGWINCH)\n91\t      - File metadata (stat, lstat; abstracts st_mtimespec/st_mtim and st_blocks divergence)\n92\t      - Process group management (setpgid, killpg) for timeout and xargs -P\n93\t      - Process priority (setpriority) for nice and renice\n94\t    references:\n95\t      - pkg/sys/\n96\t\n97\t  - name: pkg/format/\n98\t    responsibility: |\n99\t      Provides output formatting shared across utilities including table alignment,\n100\t      ANSI color output, and human-readable unit conversion (bytes to K/M/G).\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_019mhqP73ZquCVo2Eb2hfoPb","session_id":"134b6010-6885-4f08-8e6f-190349c7e60e","uuid":"5855038c-845d-4447-b39a-2120fb48957e","timestamp":"2026-05-29T15:30:53.365Z"}
