nOK, I'm confident the control chars list in the existing code is correct for macOS (just need to rename \"reprint\" to \"rprnt\").\n\nNow I'm checking the order against GNU stty's output on macOS. The existing code has the control characters in a different sequence at the end—specifically, status comes before min and time, but GNU stty places it after time. I need to reorder that section so status appears last. I'm seeing the complete list of control character mappings from coreutils, so I need to reorder the controlChars array to match: moving status after time instead of after discard, and renaming reprint to rprnt to align with the standard naming. This will also fix the default display output since it uses diffCC to show which values differ from sane defaults, and the output format needs to match what gstty produces.\n\nNow I'm checking the Go termios struct on macOS to understand the fixed size of the Cc array...\n\nWriting the code... Since `unix.Open` returns a Go error wrapping the errno, the error string should match the system strerror. Go uses the exact same system error strings on macOS.\n\nWait, actually, earlier I was worried about the case of error messages. Let me check...\n\nOn macOS, Go's syscall error for ENOTTY is defined as `Errno(0x19)`, and the `Error()` method returns lowercase strings like \"inappropriate ioctl for device\". But C's `strerror()` returns \"Inappropriate ioctl for device\" with an uppercase first letter. So there's definitely a case mismatch between what Go produces and what gstty expects.\n\nThe simplest fix is to normalize the error output in the test rather than trying to match the exact formatting, since error message differences could go beyond just capitalization. I could capitalize the first letter of Go's error message to match GNU stty's format, but normalizing stderr in the test is the safer approach. Str := strings.ToUpper(err.Error()[:1]) + err.Error()[1:]\nfmt.Fprintf(os.Stderr, \"stty: 'standard input': %s\\n\", errStr)\n```\n\nBut this feels hacky. And there might be other differences too. Let me just write the code naturally and normalize stderr in tests.\n\nActually wait, let me check if Go on macOS Darwin really uses lowercase. Let me look at the Go source code for Darwin...\n\nLooking at the Go standard library source for `syscall/zerrors_darwin_amd64.go`, I can see that Go defines error strings in lowercase, and the Errno.Error() method returns those strings as-is. So yes, Go uses lowercase error strings on Darwin, while GNU stty uses `strerror()` which on macOS gives capitalized messages like \"Inappropriate ioctl for device\".\n\nThere's definitely a case mismatch here. I could either capitalize the first letter of error messages to match GNU stty, or I could just use Go's native lowercase error strings in the binary and normalize stderr in tests to only compare exit codes for error cases.\n\nLooking at how other utilities in this repo handle this, like in `true_test.go`, they just discard stdout and don't worry about matching output exactly for version or help messages. For error cases, they probably normalize stderr too.\n\nThe SRD AC4 requirement says \"Differential test against gstty passes,\" which means our tests need to pass. If I normalize stderr and only compare exit codes for error cases, that should be fine—the actual output format matching can be tested when a terminal is available.\n\nI've been overthinking this. Let me just start implementing. I should also look at how other cmd packages handle error messages, but actually, I'll just move forward and write the implementation now. I need to handle the `onocr` flag too, which is part of the existing oflags structure.\n\nLooking at the sane field logic: flags with sane=0 aren't checked in the default display (only shown in -a mode), while sane=1 means the flag should be set in sane mode and sane=-1 means it should be unset. So `onocr` with sane=-1 means it should be unset in sane mode, and if it's set, it shows up in the default display. In -a mode, all flags are shown regardless of sane value.\n\nFor the `onocr` value itself, in a typical terminal ONOCR isn't set, and since sane=-1 means it should be unset in sane mode...\n\nTracing through the diffFlags function: it skips flags with sane=0, then for flags with sane=1 it shows them with a `-` prefix if they're not set, and for sane=-1 it shows them without the prefix if they are set.\n\nSo onocr with sane=-1 displays as \"onocr\" when set and nothing when unset, while brkint with sane=1 displays as \"-brkint\" when unset and nothing when set. This correctly shows only deviations from the sane defaults.\n\nNow I'm ready to write the implementation. I should note that uint64 is the right choice here since this targets macOS termios, and I need to reorder the cflags array to match the expected sequence: parenb, parodd, cstopb, hupcl, cread, clocal.\n\nDefining the flag structures and constants... Defining terminal flag configurations... Defining control character mappings... Looking at the rest of the display functions I need to implement, there are several helper functions for formatting terminal control characters and flags, along with utility functions for calculating window size and wrapping output. I'm estimating the total implementation will be around 100-120 lines across these various display and formatting functions.\n\nI'm considering whether some of these functions like writeAllCFlags can be inlined into the main display function to keep things simpler, and I need to think through how the wrapWriter should handle terminal width detection for the all-flags display mode, particularly when the terminal width isn't available. Writing the printAllDisplay function...\n\nNow I'm checking whether GNU stty wraps output at terminal width for the default display mode. Looking at how the default display works, it just joins the changed flags with spaces on a single line without wrapping, which matches GNU stty's behavior, so the current approach is correct.\n\nFor the implementation, I need to handle the -g restore format where the output can be passed back as arguments to restore settings. The -g format uses colons as delimiters in a single argument, so I'll check for colons in the setting string within applySettings to detect and handle the restore format.\n\nWriting the settings handler... Writing flag handling logic... Writing the bit toggle and restore functions... I'm counting up the line totals across all the functions in settings.go to make sure the file stays manageable, and it looks like everything fits well under 110 lines. Now I need to write out all the files in the right order—starting with flags.go since it has no dependencies, then moving through display.go, settings.go, main.go, and finally the test file. I also want to update that package comment in main.go to list out all the requirements we're implementing. I need to handle the error message formatting when a device file can't be opened. The unix.Open function returns lowercase error messages like \"no such file or directory\", but GNU stty outputs them capitalized. I'm writing a helper function to capitalize the first letter of error messages so they match the expected format. I'm realizing that system errors from Go's syscall package come back lowercase, but GNU utilities capitalize them. I need to decide whether to capitalize error messages in my implementation to match GNU's format, or follow whatever pattern other utilities in this repo use. Let me check how the differential tests handle stderr comparison and what approach makes sense for consistency. Handling error message formatting...\n\nI need to capitalize error messages to match GNU stty's output, and I'm also noticing that the quoting behavior differs between stdin and device arguments—'standard input' gets quoted but device paths don't. My existing code already handles this distinction correctly with the `exitTermiosErr` function. Now I'm thinking