n\nNow I'm verifying that each requirement is covered—the default behavior, the `-a` flag, the `-g` flag, the `-F` file option, and the help and version outputs all have corresponding test cases that check their exit codes.\n\nI could expand the test suite with long-form flag variants like `--all` and `--save`, negated settings like `-echo`, and combinations of multiple settings, but the core requirements are already satisfied. Let me add a few more cases to be comprehensive, then I'll write out the full test implementation.\n\nI should also verify the `-F` flag behavior—it needs to accept a device file path and operate on that terminal instead of stdin, which matches GNU stty's implementation.\n\nWriting the implementation... I'm realizing I should match GNU stty's behavior by using `TIOCSETAW` (drain then set) instead of the immediate `TIOCSETA`, since that prevents losing buffered data. Let me verify that both `TIOCSETA` and `TIOCSETAW` are available in Go's unix package for Darwin. I'm verifying that `IoctlSetTermios` accepts the ioctl constant and the termios struct, which should work fine on Darwin. The existing code already references `unix.VDSUSP` and `unix.VSTATUS`, so those constants are available, and I'll need `unix.O_NONBLOCK` and `unix.O_RDONLY` for the `-F` flag implementation.\n\nNow I'm ready to write the code. I'm organizing it into five files: flags.go for type definitions and flag arrays, display.go for all output formatting, settings.go for applying terminal settings, a rewritten main.go that orchestrates everything, and a test file for differential testing.\n\nWriting the code files... I need to finalize the package documentation structure—the `// Implements srd105-stty R1.1.` comment belongs on main.go as the package doc comment, while other files will just have the copyright header without a package comment. Now I'm writing the actual code, starting with parseArgs and making sure the long options like `--all`, `--save`, and `--file=DEVICE` are handled correctly, though I should verify whether GNU stty accepts both `--file=DEVICE` and `--file DEVICE` formats.\n\nAdding support for both `--file` argument formats...\n\nI need to match GNU stty's exact error messages for missing arguments, and handle the combination of `-F` with `-a` to display all settings for a specific device.\n\nFor the argument parsing, my approach treats unknown arguments as settings, then validates them against known flags during application—so `-echo` gets recognized as the negated \"echo\" setting. There's no conflict with option flags like `-a` or `-F` since those aren't valid setting names, though `-g` is a flag that could theoretically collide, but that's not an issue in practice.\n\nThe real problem I'm spotting is that combined arguments like `-aF` would be treated as a single setting name rather than parsed as separate flags, which diverges from how GNU stty actually handles them.\n\nLet me verify this against the actual GNU stty behavior to make sure my implementation matches... Yes, that's right—GNU stty treats `-a` and `-g` as standalone options that can't be combined with other flags, so my parser's approach is correct.\n\nNow I'm ready to start writing the implementation. Before I do, let me reconsider the file organization one more time. The guidelines mention the typical structure of `cmd/<name>/` containing `main.go` and `<name>_test.go`, but given the constraints on file and function size, I'll need to split this across multiple source files. That's a standard practice in Go, so I'm confident this is the right approach.\n\nWriting the code now...\n\nOrganizing the implementation across three files: flags.go will hold the flag definitions and constants, display.go will contain all the output formatting logic, and settings.go will handle the settings parsing. I'm starting with flags.go since it has no dependencies, then I need to think through the save format more carefully. For `printSaveFormat`, I'm outputting all the termios fields as lowercase hex separated by colons—four flag fields, twenty control character bytes, and two speed fields for a total of 26 values. When restoring, I'll parse this format back into the original uint64 and uint8 types. I'm verifying that my Go implementation using `%x` for all fields matches GNU stty's `%lx` format—both produce lowercase hex without leading zeros, so the output should be identical. For the cc values specifically, I'm casting them to unsigned long in C just like GNU stty does, which means the hex representation will be the same regardless of whether I'm formatting a uint8 or unsigned long.\n\nNow I need to consider the -g format and how the Isp field should be handled.\n\nActually, I realize the baud rate values in Go's Termios on macOS are stored as raw numbers like 9600 or 38400, which matches what GNU stty expects. On Linux they'd be B-prefixed constants, but since we're targeting macOS, the hex output will align correctly.\n\nAlright, time to actually write the code. I'll create all the files, build, and test to make sure the wrapWriter behavior works correctly for the flag lines in -a mode, where each flag category starts fresh and wraps within the column limit.\n\nWriting the implementation... So each line ends with a newline and starts fresh at column zero. But looking at actual GNU stty output, there's no blank line separating the CC section from the cflags—they flow directly into each other, which means I need to adjust my approach so writeAllCC doesn't add that trailing newline. I'm tracing through how the CC entries wrap across lines—checking column positions as each control character definition gets added, and determining when to break to a new line based on the 80-character terminal width constraint. I'm verifying my column position calculations against the actual GNU stty output to make sure my line-wrapping logic is correct. The numbers check out—after \"eol = <undef>;\" lands at column 70, the next item \"eol2 = <und